
❯ what about the Askesis tool-selection slice?

● It's the ruled-but-unscheduled follow-on, and it's your call to schedule — but here's the honest shape of it, including one fact that should weigh on the decision.

  What it is. One aggregation tool end-to-end (docs/roadmap/askesis-tool-selection-queries.md § Proposed first slice, 8 items): count_goals_achieved on GoalsBackend (parameterized, user-scoped Cypher), a
  QueryTool + typed args + one-entry catalog, LLMService.select_tool() for the Anthropic provider, a run_tool executor with server-side user_uid injection, and the AGGREGATION branch in context_retriever.py —
  which lifts PR-2's carve-out in the same commit (guard 3 and the carve-out comment both pin that pairing). The test suite is the centrepiece, especially the cross-tenant test (LLM-supplied user_uid ignored) —
  that test is the reason the slice got its own PR instead of riding in PR-2.

  Two decisions in it are yours, not build details:
  1. Coverage enforcement (its OPEN PROBLEM 1). tool_choice="auto" has no completeness check, so an unmatched count question either declines or gets the nearest tool's confident wrong number — the doc names the
     second as the dangerous failure.
  2. Delivery per answer path (ADR-077). Computing the count in ContextRetriever reaches only non-guided chat; whether an aggregation may enter the deliberately narrow Socratic prompt is a pedagogical call. The
     doc accepts a documented non-guided-only decision, but not shipping that by omission.

  The fact worth staring at before scheduling: at the 0.35 gate, all six of the ratified set's count-question shapes classify — and the first slice's single tool cleanly answers roughly none of them verbatim.
  "How many goals do I have" is a bare total, not an achieved-count; the streak, task-week, lesson-count, and predicate-bearing shapes are all outside the one tool. So the day-one, learner-visible behaviour of
  the slice as specced is mostly explicit declines with a stated reason, plus one narrow answerable shape. That's deliberate — a decline beats a confident wrong number, and the decline path is itself a tested
  deliverable — but it means the first slice is really "the safety machinery + the decline experience," not "Askesis answers count questions now." If you want the latter, the slice widens by a couple of
  bare-count tools, which grows exactly the review surface the split was meant to keep small.

  Phase fit, named honestly: this is feature building. The direction is ruled (position b, 2026-08-31), but the phase directive says paused features resume only on your explicit decision — and nothing is
  blocked meanwhile: the carve-out is stable, the fallback is the status quo, and the new log line is quietly accumulating evidence of real AGGREGATION demand, which is exactly the data that would justify (or
  deprioritize) the build.

  My recommendation: let the September review run first, and let the carve-out log accumulate a few weeks of real demand before spending the slice. If you do schedule it: rule the two decisions up front (my
  suggested minimal rulings — decline-with-reason for anything outside the catalog, non-guided path only, both documented), and it's one PR built in a fresh context per the standard arc workflow, with the
  cross-tenant test reviewed hardest.

  If you want it now instead, say so and I'll set up the fresh-context handoff prompt for it.
